    "body": "Port and terminal businesses are expanding their use of artificial intelligence , but very few can show it’s adding to earnings, according to a survey from Boston Consulting Group (BCG) and TIC4.0.\nWhat’s Related\nOnly 4% of respondents said they can point to a measurable earnings gain from AI. Only 11% said they track AI’s contribution systematically.\nForty-four percent of respondents said their organizations are expanding AI beyond early testing. That number rises to 56% at companies with more than $8 billion in revenue and drops to 32% at those under $1 billion.\nWhat's the ROI?\nUnclear return on investment topped the list of barriers at 41%. Data quality came next at 32%, followed by connecting existing systems at 30%. Implementation cost and uncertainty about carrying out projects each came in at 28%.\nThe doubt grows the further you get from the top. Twenty-one percent of C-suite leaders named unclear ROI as a problem, compared with 44% of respondents below the C-suite.\nCustomers are pushing for AI. Respondents said demand comes mostly from freight forwarders (61%), third-party logistics providers (58%), and container lines (52%). Respondents' benefits are mostly tied to work inside the terminal. Efficiency led at 74%, followed by faster decisions at 44% and cost reduction at 41%. Only 18% cited a better customer experience.\nRELATED: Why Port AI Projects Keep Running Into the Same Old Data Problem\nWhere the money is going\nHiring and training AI talent leads planned spending at 59%. Research and development comes next at 55%, then adding AI modules to existing systems at 47%. Just 2% plan no AI spending at all.\nHalf of respondents expect workers to be reskilled or moved into new jobs, while 31% expect less demand for labor .\nBased on its AI work, BCG estimates that algorithms drive 10% of value creation, technology and data 20% and training and processes 70%.\nFour moves for port leaders\nThe report lays out four steps:\nProve value before scaling . Set baselines and timelines, assign one person to each pilot to own the results, and use go/no-go checkpoints.\nFix data across the whole company. Treat data as shared infrastructure and adopt common industry data standards.\nPut AI into everyday terminal work. Focus spending on forecasting and equipment dispatch, and fund employee retraining.\nBuild commercial services for customers such as forwarders, logistics providers and liners. Examples include cargo visibility and proactive updates.\nOver the next year, BCG recommends testing a short list of AI projects against clear measures agreed on with frontline managers. Operators should also improve shared data before expanding AI into more daily tasks.",
